NAME
    gitman - interactive manager for local Git working trees

SYNOPSIS
    gitman [directory ...]

DESCRIPTION
    gitman maintains an in-memory set of local, non-bare Git working trees and
    provides interactive menus for selecting repositories and running read-only
    Git inspection commands.

    Each accepted directory is normalized to the physical top-level directory
    reported by Git for that working tree. Different paths inside the same
    working tree therefore become one entry. Distinct linked Git worktrees
    remain separate entries even when they share the same underlying Git
    repository.

    Bare repositories are not supported by this first delivery.

INITIAL DIRECTORIES
    When one or more directory operands are supplied, only those operands are
    used as the initial candidates.

    With no operands, gitman resolves:

        state-path user sys gitman conf

    and reads the optional file:

        <gitman-conf>/repositories

    The file contains one encoded directory pathname per non-empty line.

        \\n     pathname newline
        \\\\     literal backslash

    Every other character is literal. A backslash before a character other than
    n or backslash remains a literal backslash followed by that character.
    This keeps one logical directory per line while allowing pathname newlines
    and literal backslash-n text to round-trip unambiguously.

    Blank lines are ignored. Entries are not shell-evaluated: quoting,
    variables, tilde expansion and comments have no special meaning.

    Relative paths from operands and the configuration file are resolved
    relative to the directory from which gitman was invoked.

    If the file is absent or has no non-empty entries, gitman falls back to:

        .

    If the configuration pathname exists but is not a readable regular file,
    gitman records an error and also falls back to ".".

REPOSITORY VALIDATION
    A candidate is valid only when it belongs to a non-bare Git working tree.

    gitman asks Git for the working-tree top-level, resolves that top-level
    physically, and stores that physical pathname as the repository identity.
    Duplicate identities are ignored.

    Validation errors do not prevent other candidates in the same operation
    from being accepted.

ERROR FOOTER
    The bottom footer contains at most the most recent error.

    When one validation operation contains multiple invalid candidates, their
    failures are combined into one footer message. A later successful operation
    clears the previous error.

FILESYSTEM ACQUISITION
    When no valid repositories are stored, gitman opens a filesystem
    multi-selection menu rooted at the invocation directory.

        Enter       browse a directory
        Space       mark/unmark
        Backspace   parent directory
        Tab         confirm selection
        Escape      exit when the repository set is empty

    Confirmed paths are revalidated as Git working trees. If none is valid,
    acquisition is shown again with the validation error in the bottom footer.

REPOSITORY MENU
    With one or more repositories, gitman shows a single-selection repository
    menu.

        Enter   open selected repository
        a       add repositories with filesystem multi-selection
        r       remove repositories with a multi-selection list
        c       clear all repositories from the in-memory set
        s       save the current repository set to configuration
        Escape  exit gitman

    Remove and clear affect only gitman's in-memory list. They never remove
    directories or alter Git state.

    Cancelling an add/remove submenu returns to the repository menu.

SAVE CONFIGURATION
    The s action writes the current repository set to:

        <gitman-conf>/repositories

    using the configuration encoding described above.

    If the file is absent or zero-length, it is written directly. If it already
    contains data, gitman first asks whether it may be overwritten; the
    confirmation menu defaults to No. Cancelling or choosing No leaves the file
    unchanged.

    The configuration directory is created lazily when needed. A successful
    save replaces the configuration with the current repositories in menu order
    and clears the current error. A save failure leaves the in-memory repository
    set unchanged and reports the failure in the bottom footer.

GIT ACTION MENU
    Opening a repository shows these read-only actions:

        Status
            git -C <repository> status --short --branch

        Log
            git -C <repository> log --oneline --decorate --graph -n 50

        Branches
            git -C <repository> branch -vv

        Diff
            git -C <repository> diff

    Enter runs the selected action. Backspace is the default key for returning
    to the repository menu. Escape also returns to the repository menu.

    gitman preserves Git's ordinary pager-selection behavior. It does not
    force --no-pager or override GIT_PAGER, PAGER, repository configuration or
    user Git configuration.

    When Git chooses a pager, that pager runs on the restored normal terminal.
    After Git and any selected pager finish, gitman displays its explicit
    "Press any key to continue..." pause before returning to the action menu.

TERMINAL BEHAVIOR
    The menu session completes and restores its terminal state before gitman
    launches the selected Git command.

    Git therefore runs on the normal terminal, including any pager behavior
    selected by Git itself.

    When the Git command finishes, gitman displays:

        Press any key to continue...

    and waits through read-key. After one key is received, the Git action menu
    is displayed again.

    A non-zero Git command result does not end the gitman session. The Git
    command output remains visible and the action menu shows a concise failure
    in its bottom footer.

CONFIGURATION
    gitman reads the optional repositories file at startup when no directory
    operands are supplied. It writes that file only when the user explicitly
    selects the repository-menu save action; in-memory add/remove/clear changes
    are otherwise not persisted automatically.

SIDE EFFECTS
    The first delivery contains no mutating Git action. gitman does not create,
    delete or modify repositories, change branches, stage files, commit, reset,
    checkout, merge, rebase, fetch, pull, push or modify Git configuration.

EXIT STATUS
    0     Normal user exit or cancellation.
    1     Interactive/runtime execution failure.
    2     Invalid invocation.
    127   A required command is unavailable.

DEPENDENCIES
    gitman requires the RumiAI commands menu, read-key and state-path, plus an
    available Git executable.

